  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis plan introduces a two-stage optimization strategy leveraging convex relaxation and integer programming for improved convergence.\n\nWe propose:\n1.1. Relaxation stage - Solve a convex approximation using CPXOPT solver to find a lower bound.\n1.2. Integer rounding stage - Convert the relaxed solution to binary using a novel thresholding method.\n1.3. Post-processing stage - Adjust the rounded solution to maintain feasibility and minimize C\u2085.\n\nThe key innovation is our binary conversion heuristic which preserves the integral constraint while achieving near-optimal overlap properties.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nLet h \u2208 \u211d^(n_points), with h_i \u2208 [0,1], and \u2211h_i = n_points/2.\n\nObjective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k [ (\u2211h_i(1 - h_{i+k}) ) * dx ]\n\nVariables: h \u2208 \u211d^(n_points)\n\nRelaxed problem:\nMinimize C\nSubject to:\n\u2211h_i(1 - h_{i+k}) \u2264 C * dx^{-1} \u2200k\n\u2211h_i = n_points / 2\n0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n3.1. Initialization\na) If 'initial_h_values' is provided:\n   - Interpolate to n_points\n   - Normalize using h = h / sum(h) * (n_points/2)\nb) Else:\n   - Generate 3 random initializations:\n      i) Uniform: h = 0.5 * (n_points/2) / (n_points/2)\n      ii) Block: Set first n_points/2 to 1, rest to 0\n      iii) Random: h = np.random.uniform(0,1,n_points), then normalize\n\n3.2. Relaxation stage\na) Formulate the convex relaxation using CVXPY:\n   - Variable: h_relaxed \u2208 \u211d^(n_points)\n   - Constraints: h_i \u2208 [0,1], \u2211h_i = n_points/2\n   - Objective: Minimize C with \u2211h_i(1 - h_{i+k}) \u2264 C * dx^{-1} for all k\n\nb) Solve using CVXOPT with default settings\nc) Record the relaxed solution h_relaxed\n\n3.3. Integer rounding stage\na) Compute threshold t = median(h_relaxed)\nb) Convert to binary: h_candidate = 1 if h_relaxed_i > t else 0\nc) Adjust to maintain \u2211h_candidate = n_points/2:\n   i) Count deviation from 0.5\n   ii) Randomly flip h_candidate elements to correct the sum\n\n3.4. Post-processing stage\na) Define neighborhood as \u00b12% variations around h_candidate\nb) Evaluate C\u2085 for all neighbors\nc) Select the best solution within the neighborhood\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nChoose n_points=150 for better resolution while maintaining efficiency.\nSet dx=2/150\u22480.0133.\n\nFor variations:\n- Alternative rounding thresholds (mean, median, etc.)\n- Different neighborhood sizes (1%, 3%)\n- Increasing the number of initializations (5-10)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe convex relaxation dominates computation at ~40% of the budget.\nThe candidate search and evaluation consume ~30%, with post-processing ~20%.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure all h return satisfy \u2211h = n_points/2 and 0\u2264h_i\u22641.\nValidate C\u2085 using the exact correlation evaluation.",
